{"id":"W2116335100","title":"Note-taking and review: The effects of handwriting and laptop use on learning","year":2019,"abstract":"Across two experiments, we examined whether reviewing notes moderates the effects of note-taking medium on learning. Participants watched lecture videos and took notes either by hand or on a laptop, then either reviewed or did not review their notes before a delayed test. In Experiment 1, handwritten notes led to better performance than laptop notes on factual and conceptual questions, but only when participants did not review their notes. When participants reviewed their notes, the handwriting advantage was eliminated. Experiment 2 replicated this pattern and showed that review improved performance for laptop note-takers but not for handwritten note-takers. These findings suggest that reviewing notes can change the handwriting versus laptop effect."}</tool_response>{"thought": "This source directly addresses whether review changes the handwriting-laptop effect; I will also fetch the classic study to check its claims about conceptual transfer.", "action": "fetch_source", "args": {"source_id": "W2052218930"}}

